theory CompliantAttach
begin

/*
================================================================================
  SUPERSESSION, SESSIONS AND QUEUES -- what an honest serving node stops doing
================================================================================

  WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `compliant/`.  Every property here is about a node
  doing what it promised, and none of it is checkable by anyone else.
  `wire-only/attach.spthy` holds the verifiable half (endpoint authentication)
  and is explicit that it holds nothing more.

  WHAT IS BEING CHECKED.  Three obligations, written in three places, that had
  never been read against one another:

    design 12.6.5 -- "A party holding AUTHENTICATED SUPERSESSION EVIDENCE for
      a binding ... MUST NOT continue serving it: sessions under the
      superseded credential terminate, and nothing further is delivered to it,
      queued material and capture-key grants included."

    `infra-client-requirements.md` -- "Stop serving a binding you have
      verified superseded ... Fail-open is for staleness you cannot resolve,
      never for knowledge you hold."

    `light-client-requirements.md` -- "On a sibling, trust-bearing operations
      are unavailable."

  THE ONE SENTENCE THAT SHAPES THE MODEL.  design 12.6.5 says the rule
  "bites on SESSIONS AND QUEUES ALREADY RUNNING rather than on future
  lookups", because inside the horizon supersession is enforced by
  REPLACEMENT: the adoption overwrites the key, so a future lookup finds
  nothing superseded to serve.  A model that gated lookups would therefore be
  modelling the wrong thing.  What is modelled is the running state -- a live
  session and a queue with material in it -- because that is where a check is
  actually required.

  AND THE IGNORANCE CASE IS NOT A GAP.  "Outside the horizon there is no
  supersession to know, the adoption having never arrived -- those parties
  hold the old key until its attestation expires."  A node with no evidence
  that keeps serving is CORRECT, and `serving_without_evidence_is_reachable`
  asserts that trace survives.  Losing it would mean the model had started
  assuming supersession is global knowledge, which is the no-shared-state rule
  violated in the one place it is most tempting.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SETUP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE CREDENTIAL IS THE CLIENT'S, not the session's, and this matters: several
  nodes serve one client under ONE binding -- a patron and the siblings that
  stand in for it (design 14.1.2).  Minting a fresh key per session would make
  each node's credential its own and quietly destroy the only interesting
  question here, which is what happens when supersession reaches one node and
  not another.
*/

rule Client_Credential:
    [ Fr(~k) ]
  --[ Credential($C, ~k) ]->
    [ !Cred($C, ~k) ]

/*  TWO FACTS, BECAUSE THE DESIGN HAS TWO THINGS.  The node's RECORD of the
    binding -- one per (node, client, credential), entered once and retired
    once by supersession -- and the SESSIONS opened under it, as many as the
    client cares to open, each with its own role read from topology at attach
    time (design 14.1.2), each ending when it ends.  Service needs both: the
    session it runs on and the record that says the binding is still this
    node's to serve.  Supersession retires the record, so every session under
    it is inert at once -- "SESSIONS under the superseded credential
    terminate", plural (design 12.6.5) -- and no attach can follow, because an
    attach needs the record too.  A rule cannot forget to check a fact it
    needs to fire, which is why this is stronger than a flag and is the
    modelling of "enforced by replacement rather than by a check".

    TWO EARLIER SHAPES, AND WHAT EACH GOT WRONG.  The first gave each session
    its own fact and nothing else: supersession ended one session and the
    other kept serving.  The second collapsed everything into one serving
    fact per binding and added a restriction that a binding is OPENED once in
    the trace's whole history -- which did end every session at once, and
    also forbade the ordinary lifecycle outright.  design 14.1.2 has a client
    stay "on the sibling until that session ends, and the next fresh attach
    tries its actual" patron: reconnection is normal, and that model could
    not write it down.  Worse, it made the property under test true by fiat:
    a node that terminated every session and then FORGOT the credential was
    superseded would serve a reconnect, and no trace could show it because
    no reconnect was allowed.  A cross-family review found it.  Now the
    record is what the node remembers, `Session_Ends` and a fresh `Opened`
    are the reconnect, and `no_attach_after_supersession` is the claim --
    with a mutation in which supersession terminates the sessions and keeps
    the record, which is that forgetful node.

    THE RECORD IS ENTERED ONCE, by restriction, and that is not the property
    under test restated: it says a node enters a credential into its record
    once, which `currency.spthy` proves of its own record as a lemma
    (`a_key_enters_a_record_once`) and design 9.0.2's overwrite implies.  What
    keeps a superseded binding from being served is the linear record being
    gone, not this restriction; the mutation shows the difference.        */
rule Node_Records_Binding:
    [ !Cred($C, k) ]
  --[ Recorded($N, $C, k) ]->
    [ Binding($N, $C, k) ]

restriction OneRecordPerBinding:
  "All N C k #i #j. Recorded(N, C, k) @ #i & Recorded(N, C, k) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

rule Attach_Primary:
    [ Binding($N, $C, k), Fr(~s) ]
  --[ Opened($N, $C, k, ~s), ServingAs($N, $C, k, 'primary', ~s) ]->
    [ Binding($N, $C, k), Session($N, $C, k, 'primary', ~s) ]

rule Attach_Sibling:
    [ Binding($N, $C, k), Fr(~s) ]
  --[ Opened($N, $C, k, ~s), ServingAs($N, $C, k, 'sibling', ~s) ]->
    [ Binding($N, $C, k), Session($N, $C, k, 'sibling', ~s) ]

/*  A session ends -- the client goes away, the transport drops -- and the
    binding is untouched.  The next attach is a fresh session.              */
rule Session_Ends:
    [ Session($N, $C, k, role, s) ]
  --[ Ended($N, $C, k, s) ]->
    [ ]

/*  Material arrives for a client and waits.  design 14.1.4: the node holds
    ciphertext for an offline client.  THE QUEUE ENTRY NAMES THE CREDENTIAL:
    design 14.1.6, "Queue metadata is the minimum: ciphertext, recipient
    keyhash, arrival time" -- the recipient keyhash is one of the three things
    a queued item IS.  Until 2026-09-08 the queue here was per client, with no
    credential, and delivery combined an entry with whichever credential
    happened to be serving that client: an item queued under k1, k1
    superseded, k2 attached, and the item went out labelled k2 -- which the
    supersession lemma, asking about k1, never saw.  A cross-family review
    found it.  The infra requirement is "deliver nothing further to it, its
    queue included", and "it" is the credential.                            */
rule Enqueue:
    [ Fr(~m), !Cred($C, k) ]
  --[ Enqueued($N, $C, k, ~m) ]->
    [ Queued($N, $C, k, ~m) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ORDINARY SERVICE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

rule Deliver:
    [ Session($N, $C, k, role, s), Binding($N, $C, k)   // the session, and the
                                                        //   record that says this
                                                        //   binding is still
                                                        //   this node's to serve
    , Queued($N, $C, k, m) ]                            // SAME k: the item was
                                                        //   queued for this
                                                        //   credential
  --[ Delivered($N, $C, k, m), ServedUnder($N, $C, k), ServedAs($N, $C, k, role, s) ]->
    [ Session($N, $C, k, role, s), Binding($N, $C, k) ]

/*  A TRUST-BEARING operation -- a patron countersignature, the thing a
    sibling cannot do.  The guard is the serving state's own role, which is
    what `light-client-requirements.md` describes from the other end: "on a
    sibling, trust-bearing operations are unavailable".                     */
rule Trust_Bearing_Operation:
    [ Session($N, $C, k, 'primary', s), Binding($N, $C, k) ]
  --[ TrustBearing($N, $C, k, s), ServedUnder($N, $C, k), ServedAs($N, $C, k, 'primary', s) ]->
    [ Session($N, $C, k, 'primary', s), Binding($N, $C, k) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SUPERSESSION ARRIVES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The evidence is authenticated by the time it gets here -- design 12.6.5 says
  "a verified reissue chain ... a recovery it has validated", and validating it
  is `wire-only/recovery.spthy`'s subject rather than this theory's.  What is
  modelled here is the CONSEQUENCE.
*/

rule Supersession_Arrives:
    [ Binding($N, $C, k) ]             // the RECORD goes, whether or not a
                                       //   session is open; every session
                                       //   under it is inert from here, and
                                       //   no attach can follow
  --[ EvidenceHeld($N, $C, k), Terminated($N, $C, k) ]->
    [ ]                                // MUTATION (run-all.sh 3c): restore
                                       //   `Binding` here -- the node that
                                       //   ends the sessions and forgets --
                                       //   and no_attach_after_supersession
                                       //   falsifies

/*
================================================================================
  PROPERTIES
================================================================================
*/

/*  ============================================================
    NOT DISCHARGED UNBOUNDED -- but verified over a bounded model
    ============================================================

    THE CLAIMS, which are what this theory exists for:

      lemma nothing_served_under_a_credential_this_node_superseded:
        all-traces
        "All N C k #i #j.
            EvidenceHeld(N, C, k) @ #i & ServedUnder(N, C, k) @ #j
              ==> #j < #i"

      lemma nothing_delivered_after_supersession:
        all-traces
        "All N C k m #i #j.
            EvidenceHeld(N, C, k) @ #i & Delivered(N, C, k, m) @ #j
              ==> #j < #i"

    Both are left commented out because TAMARIN DOES NOT DISCHARGE THEM, and
    a lemma the gate never proves must not sit in the file looking proven.

    WHY, and it is the same cause as `compliant/currency.spthy`'s open
    obligation: `Deliver` and `Trust_Bearing_Operation` consume the binding
    record and the session and restore both, which is faithful -- serving
    ends neither -- but the backward search for the origin of
    `Binding($N,$C,k)` then regresses through unboundedly many prior
    services.  Neither lemma was FALSIFIED; there is no counterexample, only
    no proof inside 420 seconds.

    WHAT IS ESTABLISHED, and it is more than belief.  `attach.bounded`
    appends a bound -- at most one supersession, two services, two attaches
    -- and over that finite space the obligations verify (see the fragment
    for the current list and counts).
    `run-all.sh` builds that file by appending the fragment to THIS one, so
    the rules have a single source and the bounded result follows any change
    to them.  Keeping the bound in a fragment rather than in this file is also
    what preserves `no_trust_bearing_operation_on_a_sibling` as an UNBOUNDED
    result; carrying the restrictions here would have quietly demoted it.

    WHAT ELSE HOLDS HERE: the role gate, both anti-vacuity witnesses, and the
    ignorance witness, all unbounded.

    THE SAME CAUSE IN TWO THEORIES IS THE USEFUL PART.  Both obligations are
    "a node stops doing X once it knows Y", and both models express X as a
    capability the node consumes and restores.  Whatever encoding closes one
    will close the other, so this is one modelling problem rather than two.
    AND THEY ARE DISCHARGED ELSEWHERE.  `tla/SupersessionDiscipline.tla`
    checks both exhaustively over a finite instance, as safety properties of
    a mutable local state machine.  The Tamarin difficulty was isolated to a
    three-rule theory: delete the restore and the same lemma verifies in four
    steps -- a fact about the backward search, not about the claim.
    ============================================================  */

/*  A SOURCE INVARIANT, needed for the same reason `compliant/currency.spthy`
    needs one: the serving rules consume `Serving` and restore it, so a
    backward search for where the serving state came from regresses through
    unboundedly many prior services.  Naming the attach it came from closes
    the regress.                                                            */
lemma service_follows_an_attach [reuse, use_induction]:
  all-traces
  "All N C k role s #j. ServedAs(N, C, k, role, s) @ #j
      ==> Ex #i. ServingAs(N, C, k, role, s) @ #i & #i < #j"

/*  THE QUEUE FOLLOWS ITS CREDENTIAL, NOT ITS CLIENT.  Delivered under k means
    queued for k.  Unbounded, because `Queued` has exactly one origin and is
    consumed once -- no restore, no regress.  With this in hand, the bounded
    companion's "nothing delivered after supersession" covers the retag: an
    item queued under a superseded key cannot go out under its successor,
    because going out under k2 means it was queued for k2.  Deleting the
    credential match in `Deliver` falsifies this, which the gate runs.      */
lemma delivery_is_to_the_credential_it_was_queued_for:
  all-traces
  "All N C k m #j.
      Delivered(N, C, k, m) @ #j
        ==> Ex #e. Enqueued(N, C, k, m) @ #e & #e < #j"

/*  THE ROLE GATE.  A session opened in failover performs no trust-bearing
    operation.                                                              */
lemma no_trust_bearing_operation_on_a_sibling:
  all-traces
  "All N C k s #i #j.
      ServingAs(N, C, k, 'sibling', s) @ #i & TrustBearing(N, C, k, s) @ #j
        ==> F"

/*  THE LIFECYCLE THE EARLIER RESTRICTION FORBADE: a session under a binding
    ends, and a fresh one opens under the same binding.  design 14.1.2.  If
    this ever falsifies the model has gone back to one attach per binding
    and no_attach_after_supersession holds by fiat again.                  */
lemma reconnection_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C k s1 s2 #i #e #j.
      Opened(N, C, k, s1) @ #i & Ended(N, C, k, s1) @ #e
    & Opened(N, C, k, s2) @ #j & #i < #e & #e < #j & not(s1 = s2)"

/*  THE OBLIGATION THE RESTRICTION USED TO ASSUME AWAY.  Once this node holds
    supersession evidence for a binding, no attach under it follows: the
    record is gone, and an attach needs it.  This is the node REMEMBERING,
    which is what "stop serving a binding you have verified superseded" asks
    of it beyond ending the sessions it has.

    STATED IN `attach.bounded`, NOT HERE, for the cause the note above gives:
    an attach consumes the binding record and restores it, so the search for
    where the record came from regresses through unboundedly many prior
    attaches, and `[use_induction]` did not close it inside 300 seconds.  The
    bounded companion proves it over at most two attaches, one supersession
    and two services -- which is the shape of the failure it guards against,
    attach, supersede, attach -- and the mutation that keeps the record at
    supersession falsifies it there.

      lemma no_attach_after_supersession:
        all-traces
        "All N C k s #i #j.
            EvidenceHeld(N, C, k) @ #i & Opened(N, C, k, s) @ #j
              ==> #j < #i"                                                  */

/*  ANTI-VACUITY, and the reason the obligations above are not satisfied by a
    node that simply never serves anyone.                                   */
lemma delivery_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C k m #i. Delivered(N, C, k, m) @ #i"

lemma trust_bearing_operation_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C k s #i. TrustBearing(N, C, k, s) @ #i"

/*  THE IGNORANCE WITNESS.  design 12.6.5: "Outside the horizon there is no
    supersession to know ... those parties hold the old key until its
    attestation expires, which is the ignorance the table above is for and
    NOT A VIOLATION OF THIS RULE."  A second node delivering under a credential the first has
    superseded is behaving correctly, and this keeps that trace reachable.
    If it ever falsifies, the model has acquired a global supersession fact
    and broken the no-shared-state rule in the one place most tempting.    */
lemma serving_without_evidence_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N1 N2 C k m #a #b.
      EvidenceHeld(N1, C, k) @ #a
    & Delivered(N2, C, k, m) @ #b
    & #a < #b
    & not(N1 = N2)"


/*
================================================================================
  0-RTT EARLY DATA -- what a conforming server defers
================================================================================

  `wire-format.md` Section 9.1: "A server MUST NOT process an `Attach`
  received in TLS 1.3 0-RTT early data -- reject it or defer it until
  handshake [completion] ... early data is replayable, and a replayed
  `Attach` re-binds session state ... THE RULE SITS ON THE SERVER BECAUSE
  THAT IS WHERE IT IS CHECKABLE."

  WHY IT IS IN THIS TREE.  That last clause places it: checkable BY THE
  SERVER, about its own processing.  No counterparty can tell whether a
  server deferred early data or acted on it, so this is a conformance
  obligation and not a wire property.

  THE RULE IS CONNECTION-LOCAL, and until 2026-09-08 this model was not.
  "Defer it until handshake completion" means the handshake of the
  connection that carried the early data -- that is what makes deferral a
  replay defence: a replayed first flight opens a NEW connection, and the
  replayer cannot complete its handshake.  The earlier model had a
  `Handshaken(N, C)` token with no connection on it, minted by a rule with no
  premises, and a conforming server could bind early data off ANY completed
  handshake with that client, including one from before the data existed.
  The linear token did limit it to one binding per handshake -- one per
  EVENT, when the rule is one per CONNECTION.  A cross-family review found
  it.  Now a connection is a fresh `~conn`: the client opens it and sends
  its early data on it, the handshake completes on it or never, and the
  conforming server binds only with that connection's own token.

  REPLAYABILITY IS MODELLED AS PERSISTENCE, PLUS A REPLAY.  `!EarlyData` is
  persistent, so it can be consumed any number of times, and `Replay` puts
  the same early data on a new connection on which no handshake will ever
  complete -- there is no `Conn` for it, because no client opened it.  A
  conforming server therefore never binds the replay; the server that
  processes early data does.
*/

rule Client_Sends_Attach_Early:
    [ Fr(~a), Fr(~conn) ]
  --[ SentEarly($N, $C, ~conn, ~a) ]->
    [ !EarlyData($N, $C, ~conn, ~a), Conn($N, $C, ~conn) ]

/*  The attacker replays the first flight: the same early data, a new
    connection, and nobody on the client side to finish it.               */
rule Replay:
    [ !EarlyData($N, $C, conn, a), Fr(~conn2) ]
  --[ Replayed($N, $C, ~conn2, a) ]->
    [ !EarlyData($N, $C, ~conn2, a) ]

/*  The handshake completes on a connection the client opened, once.       */
rule Handshake_Completes:
    [ Conn($N, $C, conn) ]
  --[ HandshakeDone($N, $C, conn) ]->
    [ Handshaken($N, $C, conn) ]

/*  THE CONFORMING SERVER.  It has the early data -- it cannot un-receive it
    -- and binds only once THAT connection's handshake has completed,
    consuming the token that completion produced.                          */
rule Bind_After_Handshake:
    [ Handshaken($N, $C, conn), !EarlyData($N, $C, conn, a) ]
  --[ Bound($N, $C, conn, a), BoundAfterHandshake($N, $C, conn, a) ]->
    [ ]

/*  AND THE SERVER THAT PROCESSES EARLY DATA.  No handshake token consumed,
    so nothing limits how often it fires or on which connection -- which is
    the re-binding the rule exists to prevent.                              */
rule Bind_From_Early_Data:
    [ !EarlyData($N, $C, conn, a) ]
  --[ Bound($N, $C, conn, a), ProcessedEarlyData($N) ]->
    [ ]

/*  THE OBLIGATION.  On a server that defers early data, every binding
    follows the completed handshake OF THE CONNECTION THAT CARRIED IT.  The
    carve-out is this server's own: a server elsewhere that processes early
    data says nothing about N (the same scoping correction as in
    ceremony.spthy).  Letting any of C's handshakes serve -- `connT` in the
    token -- falsifies this, which the gate runs.                          */
lemma a_conforming_server_binds_only_after_the_handshake:
  all-traces
  "All N C conn a #i.
      Bound(N, C, conn, a) @ #i
      & not(Ex #s. ProcessedEarlyData(N) @ #s)
        ==> Ex #h. HandshakeDone(N, C, conn) @ #h & #h < #i"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY: a conforming server does bind.                            */
lemma a_conforming_binding_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C conn a #i.
      BoundAfterHandshake(N, C, conn, a) @ #i
    & not(Ex #s. ProcessedEarlyData(N) @ #s)"

/*  THE HARM, KEPT REACHABLE, in both its forms.  A server that acts on early
    data binds the same `Attach` twice off one replayable message with no
    second handshake -- "a replayed `Attach` re-binds session state" -- and
    binds it off a connection whose handshake never completes, which is the
    replayed one.  If either stops being reachable the model has stopped
    representing replayability and the obligation above would hold for the
    wrong reason.                                                           */
lemma a_replayed_attach_rebinds_on_a_server_that_processes_early_data:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C conn1 conn2 a #s #r #i #j.
      SentEarly(N, C, conn1, a) @ #s
    & Replayed(N, C, conn2, a) @ #r & not(conn1 = conn2)
    & Bound(N, C, conn1, a) @ #i
    & Bound(N, C, conn2, a) @ #j & ProcessedEarlyData(N) @ #j"

lemma a_server_that_processes_early_data_binds_a_connection_that_never_completes:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C conn1 conn2 a #s #r #j.
      SentEarly(N, C, conn1, a) @ #s
    & Replayed(N, C, conn2, a) @ #r & not(conn1 = conn2)
    & Bound(N, C, conn2, a) @ #j & ProcessedEarlyData(N) @ #j
    & not(Ex #h. HandshakeDone(N, C, conn2) @ #h)"

/*  BOTH WITNESSES NAME THE REPLAY.  A first version asked only for two binds
    on one connection, which the persistent `!EarlyData` supplies without
    `Replay` ever firing -- so a mutation that lost the replay transition
    would have left both controls green and the cross-connection hazard the
    wire describes unrepresented.  A cross-family review found it.  Now the
    witness is: sent on conn1, replayed onto conn2, bound on conn2 by a
    server that processes early data.                                      */


end
